## 4. Read-Write Splitting with AS OF SYSTEM TIME

**❌ NEVER: Read large payloads and write in the same transaction**

```sql
-- BAD: Transaction holds write intents during heavy read + app processing.
-- Increases duration, idle time, and 40001 probability.
BEGIN;
SELECT payload FROM documents WHERE doc_id = $1;  -- 500KB JSON
-- ... app processes the JSON for 200ms ...
UPDATE documents SET status = 'APPROVED' WHERE doc_id = $1;
COMMIT;
```

**✅ ALWAYS: Split into read → process → short write**

```sql
-- Phase 1: Conflict-free historical read (no write intents held)
SELECT payload FROM documents AS OF SYSTEM TIME '-10s' WHERE doc_id = $1;

-- Phase 2: Process in application (parse JSON, compute, decide)

-- Phase 3: Short write-only transaction
BEGIN;
UPDATE documents SET status = 'APPROVED', updated_at = now() WHERE doc_id = $1;
COMMIT;
```

`AS OF SYSTEM TIME` reads serve from the nearest replica (follower reads) — this reduces latency in multi-region deployments and eliminates read-write conflicts entirely.

