## 5. Batch Operations — Eliminate the N+1 Anti-Pattern

**❌ NEVER: Insert/update rows one at a time in a loop**

```sql
-- BAD: 500 network round trips. ~50% of transaction latency is idle/round-trip overhead.
FOR each row:
    INSERT INTO accounts (id, name, balance) VALUES (gen_random_uuid(), $name, $balance);
```

**✅ ALWAYS: Use batch operations or multi-row INSERT**

```sql
-- GOOD: Multi-row INSERT (1 round trip)
INSERT INTO accounts (id, name, balance) VALUES
    (gen_random_uuid(), 'Alice', 1000.00),
    (gen_random_uuid(), 'Bob', 2000.00),
    (gen_random_uuid(), 'Carol', 3000.00);

-- GOOD: Batch size of 128-256 rows per statement for optimal throughput
-- GOOD: Use JDBC addBatch/executeBatch, Python executemany, etc.
```

For batch UPDATEs, use a single statement instead of N statements:

```sql
-- GOOD: Single-statement batch update
UPDATE accounts SET balance = CASE account_id
    WHEN 'uuid-1' THEN 100.00
    WHEN 'uuid-2' THEN 200.00
    WHEN 'uuid-3' THEN 300.00
END WHERE account_id IN ('uuid-1', 'uuid-2', 'uuid-3');
```

