## 8. Indexing Strategies

### 8a. Covering Indexes with STORING

**✅ ALWAYS: Add STORING for columns returned by queries using the index**

```sql
-- BAD: Requires an "index join" back to the primary index to get name and balance
CREATE INDEX idx_status ON accounts (status);

-- GOOD: Covering index — all columns served from the secondary index
CREATE INDEX idx_status ON accounts (status) STORING (customer_name, balance);
```

`STORING` is CockroachDB's equivalent of PostgreSQL's `INCLUDE`. It embeds non-key columns in the index to avoid a round-trip to the primary index.

### 8b. Partial Indexes

**✅ Use partial indexes for frequently filtered subsets**

```sql
-- GOOD: Only indexes ACTIVE rows — smaller size, less write amplification
CREATE INDEX idx_active_accounts ON accounts (customer_name)
    WHERE status = 'ACTIVE';

-- GOOD: Only indexes pending orders
CREATE INDEX idx_pending_orders ON orders (created_at)
    WHERE status = 'PENDING';
```

### 8c. GIN (Inverted) Indexes on JSONB

**✅ ALWAYS: Add GIN indexes on JSONB columns used in containment queries**

```sql
-- GOOD: Enables fast @> queries without full-table scans
CREATE INVERTED INDEX idx_metadata ON accounts (metadata);

-- Now this is fast:
SELECT * FROM accounts WHERE metadata @> '{"tier": "PLATINUM"}';
SELECT * FROM accounts WHERE metadata @> '{"tags": ["premium"]}';
```

### 8d. Computed Columns from JSONB

**✅ Extract frequently queried JSON fields into typed, indexed columns**

```sql
-- GOOD: Typed column with B-tree index for fast range queries
ALTER TABLE accounts ADD COLUMN risk_score INT
    AS ((metadata->>'risk_score')::INT) STORED;
CREATE INDEX idx_risk_score ON accounts (risk_score);

-- Now range queries are fast:
SELECT * FROM accounts WHERE risk_score > 80;
```

### 8e. Expression Indexes

**✅ Use expression indexes when you don't need a stored column**

```sql
-- GOOD: Index the expression result directly
CREATE INDEX idx_tier ON accounts ((metadata->>'tier'));

-- Now this uses the index:
SELECT * FROM accounts WHERE metadata->>'tier' = 'GOLD';
```

### 8f. Hash-Sharded Indexes for Sequential Keys

**✅ Use hash-sharded indexes when sequential ordering creates hotspots**

```sql
-- GOOD: Distributes writes across 8 buckets
CREATE INDEX idx_ts ON events (created_at) USING HASH WITH (bucket_count = 8);
```

Trade-off: range scans must fan out across all buckets.

### Index Maintenance

```sql
-- Audit unused indexes (they add write overhead):
SELECT * FROM crdb_internal.index_usage_statistics WHERE total_reads = 0;

-- Every foreign key column SHOULD have an index
-- Drop indexes that are never read
```

> Docs: https://www.cockroachlabs.com/docs/stable/schema-design-indexes#best-practices
> Hash-sharded: https://www.cockroachlabs.com/docs/stable/hash-sharded-indexes
> Partial: https://www.cockroachlabs.com/docs/stable/partial-indexes
> GIN: https://www.cockroachlabs.com/docs/stable/inverted-indexes

